<p>I started with HTML and CSS over 10 years ago and spent most of my career building large-scale React apps.
  Accessibility, performance and a strong foundation in the basics shaped how I think about software. Over time, I moved
  across the whole stack and delivered features end to end, from the front-end to the Kotlin backend.</p>

<p>At Ataccama, I work as an AI engineer and lead the AI circle &mdash; an initiative to re-invent how our engineering
  organization builds software with AI. Generating code is the easy part. The hard part is knowing the output is correct
  and maintainable. So I spend my time on agentic harnesses, skills, evals and quality gates.</p>

<p>Right now, the biggest bottleneck is code review. Agents produce more merge requests than people can review, so I
  build tooling that tells reviewers how much attention each change really needs (a small UI tweak vs. a change to
  shared infrastructure or security). My <a href="https://github.com/Pustelto/code-review-skills">code review skill</a>
  ranks 2nd of 44 tools on the Martian Code Review Bench and is one of the most used skills in our company. I also join
  one team at a time and help them set up guardrails (lint rules, tests, review checks) so they can ship high-quality
  code with agents &mdash; even in parts of the stack they don't know well.</p>

<p>I like to share what I learn. I organize knowledge-sharing sessions on AI across our engineering teams and I spoke
  about <a href="/talks/">prompt engineering for humans</a> at Frontkon 2025.</p>

<p>I have also built two solo products, <a href="https://clipio.app">Clipio</a> and Qjub. I drove everything from market
  research and design to backend, payments and marketing. Neither became a sustainable business, but both pushed me far
  outside my comfort zone (and taught me more than any course would).</p>

<p>In addition to my work, I enjoy spending time with my family (I'm a father of three boys) and hobbies such as being
  in nature, reading, and enjoying great tea. I'm also a great productivity and tooling geek.</p>

